🔹 IPv4 Address Types
Private IPv4 Addresses

Private addresses are used inside local networks and are not directly routable on the public Internet.

Range	CIDR
10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255	10.0.0.0/8
172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255	172.16.0.0/12
192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255	192.168.0.0/16

Example:

192.168.1.20

Public IPv4 Addresses

Public IP addresses are globally routable and are used for communication across the Internet.

Example:

8.8.8.8

🔹 IPv6 Address Types

IPv6 does not use IPv4-style RFC1918 private addresses.

Common IPv6 address types include:

Type	Range / Example	Purpose
Global Unicast	2000::/3	Public Internet communication
Link-Local	fe80::/10	Communication on local network segment
Unique Local	fc00::/7	Private/internal networks
Loopback	::1	Local host
Unspecified	::	No address assigned
Multicast	ff00::/8	One-to-many communication
🔹 Private IPv4 vs IPv6 Unique Local Address

IPv4 commonly uses:

192.168.1.10

IPv6 can use a Unique Local Address (ULA), for example:

fd12:3456:789a::10

Both can be used for internal/private network communication.

🔹 Important IPv4 → IPv6 Differences
1. Address Size

IPv4:

192.168.1.1

32-bit address.

IPv6:

2001:db8:abcd:0012::1

128-bit address.

2. NAT

IPv4 commonly uses NAT because IPv4 addresses are limited.

IPv6 provides such a large address space that devices can generally have globally unique addresses without requiring NAT.

Note: IPv6 can still use NAT in some environments, but it is not required for address conservation in the way it commonly is with IPv4.

3. Broadcast

IPv4 supports broadcast.

Example:

255.255.255.255

IPv6 does not use broadcast.

Instead, IPv6 uses multicast for many functions.

4. ARP vs NDP

IPv4 uses:

ARP – Address Resolution Protocol

IPv6 uses:

NDP – Neighbor Discovery Protocol

NDP operates using ICMPv6.

5. Address Configuration

IPv4 commonly uses:

Static configuration
DHCP

IPv6 can use:

Static configuration
SLAAC
DHCPv6

SLAAC = Stateless Address Autoconfiguration

This allows an IPv6 device to automatically configure its address using information provided by the network.

6. Fragmentation

IPv4 allows fragmentation by routers and hosts.

IPv6 routers do not fragment packets.

The source host is responsible for fragmentation when required.

7. Header

IPv4 has a variable header size, with a minimum of 20 bytes.

IPv6 has a fixed base header size of 40 bytes and uses extension headers for additional functionality.

🔹 Special Addresses
IPv4 Loopback
127.0.0.1

Used to test the local machine.

IPv6 Loopback
::1

Equivalent concept to IPv4 127.0.0.1.

IPv4 Unspecified
0.0.0.0
IPv6 Unspecified
::
🧠 Key CCNA Takeaways
IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses.
IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses.
IPv4 addresses are written in decimal.
IPv6 addresses are written in hexadecimal.
IPv4 supports broadcast.
IPv6 does not use broadcast.
IPv4 uses ARP.
IPv6 uses NDP.
IPv4 commonly uses NAT.
IPv6 generally does not require NAT for address conservation.
IPv4 uses DHCP commonly.
IPv6 supports SLAAC and DHCPv6.
IPv4 can be fragmented by routers.
IPv6 routers do not fragment packets.
IPv4 private ranges are 10/8, 172.16/12, and 192.168/16.
IPv6 uses Unique Local Addresses (ULA) for private/internal addressing.
IPv6 provides a vastly larger address space.
